DDEESSCCRRIIPPTTIIOONN
     zztteerrmm opens an 80x25-character terminal window under the
     zzvviieeww desktop and runs an interactive /bbiinn/sshh in it on a
     pseudo-terminal, with full job control: ^C interrupts, ^\
     quits, and the line discipline is the normal tty one.  The
     window closes by itself when the shell exits.  Quitting the
     window from its menu asks for confirmation first, then hangs
     up the shell.  zztteerrmm can be started from a desktop icon or
     typed at a shell inside another zztteerrmm; it accepts the stan-
     dard window _o_p_t_i_o_n_s of GUI applications (though not -SS: the
     size is fixed); see zzvviieeww.

     Keystrokes go to the shell.  The hi-res keyboard delivers
     plain ASCII, and its special keys produce control codes
     rather than escape sequences:

                UUpp, DDoowwnn          ^P, ^N
                LLeefftt, RRiigghhtt       ^B, ^F
                HHOOMMEE, EENNDD         ^A, ^E
                PPGG UUPP, PPGG DDNN      ^Z, ^V
                BBSS                ^H (0x08)
                DDEELL, kkeeyyppaadd DDeell   DEL (0x7f)
                FF1100               ^X ^C (two keys)

     The remaining function keys produce codes with no byte value
     and are ignored in a terminal window.

     On the output side zztteerrmm emulates a deliberate subset of the
     ANSI terminal, enough for the shell, simple full-screen
     programs and llss: CR, LF, BS, TAB (8-column stops), and the
     escape sequences EESSCC [ rr;ccHH (and ff) cursor position, EESSCC [
     nnAA/BB/CC/DD cursor motion, EESSCC [ JJ clear screen and home, and
     EESSCC [ KK clear to end of line.  There are no character at-
     tributes: bold, reverse and colour are not rendered.  Other
     escape sequences are swallowed silently.

     Up to 200 lines that scroll off the top are kept and can be
     viewed with the scrollbar on the left edge; any shell output
     snaps the view back to the live screen.  A left drag selects
     text (wrapped lines are joined, trailing blanks trimmed) and
     publishes it as the PRIMARY selection on release; a middle
     click in any terminal pastes the PRIMARY selection to the
     shell.  The window menu's CCooppyy copies the PRIMARY selection
     to the clipboard, and PPaassttee types the clipboard to the
     shell.

